{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "What is the objective of this packing plan — are we minimizing the number of containers used, maximizing the total tons of goods shipped, or something else (e.g., maximizing the number of units shipped, or meeting a required shipment quantity at minimum cost)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the optimization objective, presenting multiple options for one decision."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Do we have to ship all available goods (i.e., every unit of A, B, C, D, E must be packed into some container), or is it allowed to leave some goods unshipped as long as the containers that are used satisfy the rules?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether all goods must be shipped or if some can be left unshipped."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Is there a fixed fleet of containers available (i.e., a maximum number of containers we can use), or can we use as many containers as needed as long as each used container satisfies the 18–60 ton and 12-unit-D rules?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy choice regarding the availability of containers: either a fixed maximum limit exists, or the quantity is unlimited subject to per-container constraints. This is one semantically independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "does this apply per container (i.e., each individual container that has at least one A unit must also have at least one C unit), or is it a global rule across all containers (i.e., as long as the total shipment includes at least one C, A can be shipped)?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy clarification regarding the scope of the rule (per-container vs. global), which constitutes one independent question."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "The minimum of 12 units of type D per container — does this apply to every container that is used, or only to containers that actually contain type D goods?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response contains a single clarification question asking for a specific policy scope definition."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Are the goods units indivisible (i.e., each unit must be assigned whole to a single container, so the number of units of each type in each container must be a non-negative integer), or can units be split fractionally across containers?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding the integrality constraint of the units, presenting a binary choice between indivisible and fractional assignment."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since we can use as many containers as needed and are allowed to leave goods unshipped, is there any requirement that at least one container must be used (i.e., we must ship at least some goods), or is it acceptable for the optimal solution to use zero containers and ship nothing?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether the solution must use at least one container or if zero containers are allowed."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Since we are minimizing the number of containers and can leave goods unshipped, is there a required minimum amount of goods that must be shipped (e.g., a minimum total tonnage or a minimum number of units of a specific type), or is the only requirement that each container we do use satisfies the 18–60 ton and 12-unit-D rules?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks a single clarification question regarding whether there is a global minimum shipment requirement or if only per-container constraints apply. Although it presents two alternatives, it seeks a single policy decision."
}

{
  "action": "question",
  "question_count": 1,
  "is_atomic": true,
  "atomic_questions": [
    "Regarding the per-container weight limits, is the 60-ton maximum a hard capacity constraint (a container can never exceed 60 tons), and is the 18-ton minimum a hard requirement (a used container can never be loaded below 18 tons), or is either of these a soft target that could be relaxed with a penalty?"
  ],
  "rationale": "The response asks for a single policy classification regarding the nature of the weight limits (hard constraint vs. soft target with penalty). Although it mentions both the maximum and minimum, it frames them as a single choice between 'hard' and 'soft' for the limits collectively, which constitutes one semantic decision point."
}

{
  "action": "question",
